LUAC(1)                        P32W Unix                         LUAC(1)

NAME
       luac - Lua compiler

SYNOPSIS
       luac [options] [files]

DESCRIPTION
       luac translates Lua programs into binary chunks (Lua's
       bytecode), which lua(1) loads like the source but without
       parsing it. The chunks of all the files are joined into one
       that runs them in the order given; - is the standard input.
       By default the result is luac.out.

       A binary chunk has the same meaning as its source, but it
       only loads on a machine with the same word sizes and number
       types: P32W's (32-bit integers, double floats, little-endian)
       are not the usual 64-bit Lua's, so chunks do not move between
       P32W and other machines; bring the source instead. Errors in a
       binary chunk can make lua misbehave rather than report them.

OPTIONS
       -l     list the bytecode (-l -l: also the constants, locals
              and upvalues)
       -o file
              write to file (- is the standard output)
       -p     only parse (check the syntax): no output file
       -s     strip the debugging information (line numbers, names)
       -v     print the version
       --     stop handling options
       -      the standard input is a file and the options end

EXAMPLES
       luac -o prog.luac prog.lua; lua prog.luac
       luac -p *.lua                  check the syntax only
       luac -s -o - prog.lua | lua -

FILES
       luac.out
              the default output

EXIT STATUS
       0, or 1 after an error.

SEE ALSO
       lua(1); "Lua 5.4 Reference Manual" (www.lua.org). Lua is by
       R. Ierusalimschy, W. Celes and L. H. de Figueiredo, Lua.org,
       PUC-Rio; MIT licence.

BUGS
       Like lua, it must fit in an 8 MiB process: a source file of
       some thousands of lines is fine. The listing's addresses
       (0x...) are where the functions happened to be in memory.
